OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026", "title": "Strict hot spots and absence of interior critical points on smooth simply connected planar domains", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026/main.pdf", "family": "369", "family_title": "The hot spots conjecture for simply connected planar domains", "abstract_html": "We prove the strict hot spots conjecture for smooth bounded simply connected planar domains. More precisely, every nonzero eigenfunction for the first positive Neumann eigenvalue has nonvanishing gradient in the interior, so all its global maxima and minima lie on the boundary. This holds even when the eigenvalue is multiple.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) be a nonempty bounded simply connected open set with \\(C^\\infty\\) boundary.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the first positive Neumann eigenvalue by\n  \\[\n  \\mu=\\mu_1(\\Omega)\n  =\n  \\inf_{\\substack{0\\ne v\\in H^1(\\Omega)\\\\ \\int_\\Omega v=0}}\n  \\frac{\\int_\\Omega|\\nabla v|^2}{\\int_\\Omega|v|^2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\) be the real Neumann eigenspace for \\(\\mu\\):\n  \\[\n  V=\n  \\left\\{\n  v\\in H^1(\\Omega):\n  \\int_\\Omega\\nabla v\\cdot\\nabla\\psi\n  =\n  \\mu\\int_\\Omega v\\psi\n  \\text{ for every }\\psi\\in H^1(\\Omega)\n  \\right\\}.\n  \\]\n  Its elements are smooth up to the boundary by elliptic regularity.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(0\\ne u\\in V\\),\n\\[\n\\nabla u(x)\\ne0\\qquad(x\\in\\Omega).\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\min_{y\\in\\partial\\Omega}u(y)\n&lt;\nu(x)\n&lt;\n\\max_{y\\in\\partial\\Omega}u(y)\n\\qquad(x\\in\\Omega).\n\\]\nThe assertion applies to every nonzero member of \\(V\\), including when \\(\\mu\\) is multiple.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take an arbitrary \\(0\\ne u\\in V\\), and set\n\\[\ng(s)=(\\nabla u)(\\Phi(s)).\n\\]\nSuppose \\(\\Phi(p)\\) is a critical point. Proposition 8 supplies an injective Lipschitz map \\(b:S^1\\to H\\), with coordinates \\(b_j\\), and functions \\(v_j\\in V\\) whose boundary gradients, pulled back by \\(\\Phi\\), are \\(b_jg\\). The boundary gradient of \\(u\\) is \\(g\\).</p><p>Set\n\\[\nS_g=\\{s\\in S^1:g(s)\\ne0\\}.\n\\]\nThis is a nonempty open set. If it were empty, each Cartesian derivative of \\(u\\) would solve the Helmholtz equation with zero Dirichlet trace, contradicting \\(\\mu&lt;\\lambda_D\\) from Proposition 1 unless both vanished. Then \\(u\\) would be a constant eigenfunction for a positive eigenvalue, hence zero.</p><p>The two possibilities for \\(S_g\\) lead respectively to multiplicity and boundary-uniqueness contradictions.</p><p>**Case 1: \\(S_g\\) is dense.**</p><p>The set \\(b(S_g)\\) cannot lie in an affine line. Otherwise continuity would place \\(b(S^1)\\) in that closed line, but a continuous injective circle cannot map into a line. Indeed its compact connected image would be an interval; deleting an interior point disconnects an interval but not a circle with one point removed.</p><p>Choose three points of \\(S_g\\) whose images are affinely independent. Their two difference vectors are linearly independent in \\(H\\), so some two coordinates \\(j,k\\) have a nonzero \\(2\\times2\\) minor. To see this, if all such minors vanished, a nonzero coordinate of the first difference vector would force every coordinate of the second to be proportional to it.</p><p>Thus the functions \\(1,b_j,b_k\\) are linearly independent on those three points. Since \\(g\\ne0\\) there, the vector traces\n\\[\ng,\\qquad b_jg,\\qquad b_kg\n\\]\nare linearly independent. They are gradient traces of three functions in \\(V\\), contradicting \\(\\dim V\\le2\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p>**Case 2: \\(S_g\\) is not dense.**</p><p>There is a nonempty open arc \\(I\\) on which \\(g=0\\). Since \\(S_g\\) is nonempty and open and \\(b\\) is injective, some coordinate \\(b_j\\) is nonconstant on \\(S_g\\). Consequently \\(g\\) and \\(b_jg\\) are linearly independent, and so are their eigenfunctions \\(u\\) and \\(v_j\\).</p><p>Their gradients vanish on the boundary arc \\(\\Phi(I)\\), so each has a constant trace there. Choose a nonzero linear combination \\(w\\) of them whose constant trace on that arc is zero. It also has zero normal derivative there.</p><p>Take a small neighborhood of an interior point of the arc that meets no other boundary portion, and extend \\(w\\) by zero to the exterior of \\(\\Omega\\) in that neighborhood. Integration by parts against compactly supported tests shows that this extension solves\n\\[\n(\\Delta+\\mu)w=0\n\\]\ndistributionally: both its Dirichlet and normal traces on the intervening boundary vanish.</p><p>Interior elliptic regularity and analyticity make the extension analytic. Since it is zero on an exterior open set, it is zero throughout a smaller neighborhood. Analytic continuation inside the connected domain gives \\(w=0\\) on \\(\\Omega\\), contradicting independence of \\(u\\) and \\(v_j\\). No analyticity of the boundary is used.</p><p>Both cases contradict the assumed critical point. Thus \\(\\nabla u\\) is nowhere zero in \\(\\Omega\\).</p><p>The continuous function \\(u\\) attains both extrema on the compact closure. Any interior extremum would be critical, so both are attained on the boundary. An interior value equal to either boundary extremum would itself be a global extremum. Hence\n\\[\n\\min_{y\\in\\partial\\Omega}u(y)\n&lt;\nu(x)\n&lt;\n\\max_{y\\in\\partial\\Omega}u(y)\n\\qquad(x\\in\\Omega).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathbb D=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|&lt;1\\}\\), and let \\(S^1=\\partial\\mathbb D\\), equipped with arclength \\(ds\\), of total mass \\(2\\pi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(dq=a\\,dx\\), where \\(a\\in L^\\infty(\\mathbb D)\\) is nonnegative. Suppose that some \\(0&lt;d&lt;1\\) satisfies\n  \\[\n  \\int_{\\mathbb D}|\\psi|^2\\,dq\n  \\le\n  d\\int_{\\mathbb D}|\\nabla\\psi|^2\n  \\qquad(\\psi\\in H_0^1(\\mathbb D)).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define the Dirichlet Green function and Poisson kernel by\n  \\[\n  G(x,y)\n  =\n  \\frac1{2\\pi}\\log\\left|\\frac{1-x\\overline y}{x-y}\\right|\n  =\n  \\frac1{4\\pi}\n  \\log\\left(\n  1+\\frac{(1-|x|^2)(1-|y|^2)}{|x-y|^2}\n  \\right),\n  \\]\n  with \\(G(x,x)=+\\infty\\), and\n  \\[\n  P_s(x)=\\frac{1-|x|^2}{2\\pi|s-x|^2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  Tf(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb D}G(x,y)f(y)\\,dq(y).\n  \\]\n  For \\(p\\in\\mathbb D\\) and distinct \\(s,t\\in S^1\\), define\n  \\[\n  K(p,s)=P_s(p)+\\sum_{n\\ge1}(T^nP_s)(p),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  R(s,t)=\n  \\sum_{n\\ge0}\\int_{\\mathbb D}P_s\\,T^nP_t\\,dq,\n  \\qquad\n  N(s,t)=\\frac1{\\pi|s-t|^2}+R(s,t).\n  \\]\n  Powers applied to \\(P_s\\) mean iterated nonnegative kernel integrals, not an a priori application of an \\(L^2(q)\\) operator to \\(P_s\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A symmetric zero-diagonal kernel \\(D\\) is conditionally negative semidefinite if\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{i,j}a_i a_jD(s_i,s_j)\\le0\n  \\]\n  for every finite family of points \\(s_i\\) and real coefficients \\(a_i\\) with \\(\\sum_i a_i=0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(p\\in\\mathbb D\\), the kernel\n\\[\nD_p(s,t)=\\frac{K(p,s)K(p,t)}{N(s,t)}\n\\quad(s\\ne t),\n\\qquad\nD_p(s,s)=0\n\\]\nis conditionally negative semidefinite on \\(S^1\\).</p><p>For \\(q=0\\), the observation in original Remark 5.2 is\n\\[\nD_p(s,t)\n=\n\\pi P_s(p)P_t(p)|s-t|^2\n=\n\\frac1{4\\pi}|\\varphi_p(s)-\\varphi_p(t)|^2,\n\\qquad\n\\varphi_p(z)=\\frac{z-p}{1-\\overline pz}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The kernel constructions and estimates required by the statement hold for the general disk potential by Proposition 3.</p><p>Fix distinct points \\(s_1,\\ldots,s_m\\in S^1\\) and real coefficients \\(a_i\\) with\n\\[\n\\sum_i a_i=0.\n\\]\nUse the matrix \\(B\\) and vector \\(\\ell\\) of Proposition 7, and put\n\\[\nx_i=K(p,s_i)a_i.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\ell^Tx=\\sum_i a_i=0.\n\\]\nProposition 7 therefore gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{i,j}a_i a_jD_p(s_i,s_j)\n=\nx^TBx\n\\le0.\n\\]\nThis proves the assertion on distinct points; repeated points are handled by combining their coefficients.</p><p>For \\(q=0\\), the defining formulas give \\(K(p,s)=P_s(p)\\) and \\(N=N_0\\), yielding the expression recorded in original Remark 5.2:\n\\[\nD_p(s,t)\n=\n\\pi P_s(p)P_t(p)|s-t|^2\n=\n\\frac1{4\\pi}|\\varphi_p(s)-\\varphi_p(t)|^2.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Proposition 2.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Omega,\\mu,V\\) be as in Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\lambda_D\n  =\n  \\inf_{0\\ne\\psi\\in H_0^1(\\Omega)}\n  \\frac{\\int_\\Omega|\\nabla\\psi|^2}{\\int_\\Omega|\\psi|^2}\n  \\]\n  be the first Dirichlet eigenvalue.</p><p class=\"li\">Both Rayleigh infima are attained, and their eigenspaces are finite-dimensional, by Poincaré inequalities and Sobolev compactness.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following preliminary conclusions hold:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(\\mu&lt;\\lambda_D\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. There is a conformal bijection \\(\\Phi:\\mathbb D\\to\\Omega\\) extending to a smooth diffeomorphism of closures, with \\(\\Phi'\\ne0\\) on \\(\\mathbb D\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The real eigenspace \\(V\\) has dimension at most two.</p>", "proof": "<p>The spectral separation and conformal parametrization are Lemmas 1.1 and 1.2. It remains to prove the multiplicity bound, the assertion of original Proposition 2.5.</p><p>Suppose \\(\\dim V\\ge3\\). Since the trace map is injective by Lemma 1.3, choose \\(0\\ne v\\in V\\) whose trace \\(h=h_v\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\int_{S^1}\\beta h\\cos\\theta\\,ds\n=\n\\int_{S^1}\\beta h\\sin\\theta\\,ds\n=\n0,\n\\qquad s=e^{i\\theta}.\n\\]\nIt is already orthogonal to \\(1\\) by Lemma 1.3.</p><p>Cut the circle at a strictly negative point and write its angles as \\((\\theta_0,\\theta_0+2\\pi)\\). If \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the infimum and supremum of the positive angles, then\n\\[\n\\theta_0&lt;a&lt;b&lt;\\theta_0+2\\pi.\n\\]\nThere are no positive values outside \\([a,b]\\). Nor is there a negative value between \\(a\\) and \\(b\\): positive values on either side of it, together with the cut point, would violate Lemma 1.4.</p><p>The function\n\\[\nF(\\theta)\n=\n\\cos\\left(\\theta-\\frac{a+b}{2}\\right)\n-\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{b-a}{2}\\right)\n\\]\nis strictly positive on \\((a,b)\\) and negative outside \\([a,b]\\) in the cut interval. Consequently \\(Fh\\ge0\\) everywhere and \\(Fh&gt;0\\) on a nonempty open set. Thus\n\\[\n\\int_{S^1}\\beta Fh\\,ds&gt;0,\n\\]\ncontradicting the three orthogonalities. Therefore \\(\\dim V\\le2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the domain and eigenvalues of Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mu&lt;\\lambda_D.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a nonzero nonnegative Dirichlet minimizer \\(\\phi\\), using absolute values in the Rayleigh quotient. Then \\(\\int_\\Omega\\phi&gt;0\\). Work temporarily over \\(\\mathbb C\\). For \\(k\\in\\mathbb R^2\\) with \\(|k|^2=\\lambda_D\\), put\n\\[\ne_k(x)=e^{ik\\cdot x},\n\\qquad\nw_k=e_k-\\frac{\\int_\\Omega e_k}{\\int_\\Omega\\phi}\\phi.\n\\]\nEach \\(w_k\\) has mean zero.</p><p>The form\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega\\bigl(|\\nabla v|^2-\\lambda_D|v|^2\\bigr)\n\\]\nvanishes on \\(e_k\\) and \\(\\phi\\); its cross term vanishes because\n\\(-\\Delta e_k=\\lambda_De_k\\) and \\(\\phi\\) has zero trace. Thus every nonzero \\(w_k\\) has Rayleigh quotient \\(\\lambda_D\\), proving \\(\\mu\\le\\lambda_D\\).</p><p>If equality held, each such \\(w_k\\) would be a Neumann eigenfunction by variational equality. The Euler equation first holds against mean-zero tests and then against all tests, since \\(w_k\\) has mean zero. But distinct plane waves are linearly independent: restrict any finite family to a line segment in an interior ball whose direction gives distinct frequencies. Subtracting multiples of one fixed function leaves their span infinite-dimensional. This contradicts finite eigenspace dimension.</p><p>The real Neumann inequality and its equality case apply in the complexified space by separating real and imaginary parts.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(\\Omega\\) be the smooth bounded simply connected planar domain of Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a conformal bijection\n\\[\n\\Phi:\\mathbb D\\longrightarrow\\Omega\n\\]\nextending to a smooth diffeomorphism of closures, with \\(\\Phi'\\ne0\\) on \\(\\mathbb D\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Identify the plane with \\(\\mathbb C\\) and fix \\(a\\in\\Omega\\). Let \\(l\\) solve the harmonic Dirichlet problem with boundary values \\(\\log|z-a|\\). It is smooth up to the boundary. Simple connectivity gives a harmonic conjugate \\(\\widetilde l\\); its gradient extends smoothly, so the conjugate also extends smoothly in boundary charts.</p><p>The holomorphic function\n\\[\nm(z)=(z-a)\\exp\\bigl(-l(z)-i\\widetilde l(z)\\bigr)\n\\]\nhas modulus one on the boundary, modulus less than one inside, and precisely one zero, of order one. Near the boundary, \\(\\log|m|\\) is harmonic, negative inside, and zero on the boundary. The boundary point lemma gives a nonzero normal derivative, so \\(m'\\) does not vanish there.</p><p>For any \\(w\\in\\mathbb D\\), take a smooth inner approximation of \\(\\Omega\\) so close to its boundary that \\(|m|&gt;|w|\\) throughout the omitted collar. Rouché’s theorem gives exactly one zero of \\(m-w\\) in that approximation, counting multiplicity, because \\(m\\) has exactly one. There are none in the collar. Thus \\(m\\) is a biholomorphism in the interior.</p><p>It maps the boundary onto the circle by compactness. Its local boundary inverses imply injectivity there: two boundary preimages would give two nearby interior preimages. The inverse map is the required \\(\\Phi\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\Omega,\\mu,V\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the conformal parametrization \\(\\Phi\\) supplied by Lemma 1.2.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(v\\in V\\), write\n  \\[\n  h_v=(v\\circ\\Phi)|_{S^1}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a fixed smooth positive function \\(\\beta\\) on \\(S^1\\) such that\n\\[\n\\int_{S^1}\\beta h_v\\,ds=0\n\\qquad(v\\in V).\n\\]\nIf \\(v\\ne0\\), its boundary trace is nonzero and takes both signs.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma 1.1, the Dirichlet problem\n\\[\n(-\\Delta-\\mu)L=1,\n\\qquad\nL|_{\\partial\\Omega}=0\n\\]\nis coercive and has a smooth solution. Testing by its negative part gives \\(L\\ge0\\). Hence\n\\[\n-\\Delta L=1+\\mu L&gt;0,\n\\]\nand the boundary point lemma gives \\(-\\partial_\\nu L&gt;0\\).</p><p>Green’s identity and the Neumann equation yield\n\\[\n0=\\int_\\Omega v\n=\n-\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}v\\,\\partial_\\nu L\n=\n\\int_{S^1}\\beta(s)h_v(s)\\,ds,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\beta=|\\Phi'|\\,(-\\partial_\\nu L)\\circ\\Phi.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(h_v=0\\), then \\(v\\in H_0^1(\\Omega)\\), and the Dirichlet inequality and \\(\\mu&lt;\\lambda_D\\) force \\(v=0\\). Positivity of \\(\\beta\\) gives the last assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma 2.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(\\Omega,\\mu,V,\\Phi\\) and \\(h_v\\) from Proposition 1 and Lemma 1.3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(0\\ne v\\in V\\), the sets\n\\[\n\\{v&gt;0\\},\\qquad \\{v&lt;0\\}\n\\]\nare connected. Its boundary trace \\(h_v\\) cannot have four cyclically ordered points with strictly alternating signs.</p>", "proof": "<p>For each component \\(U\\) of a sign set, the function\n\\[\nv_U=v\\,1_U\n\\]\nbelongs to \\(H^1(\\Omega)\\) and has weak gradient \\(1_U\\nabla v\\). Indeed it is locally Lipschitz in interior balls: any transition between \\(U\\) and its complement crosses a zero of \\(v\\), so the local Lipschitz bound for \\(v\\) also bounds the zeroed function. This gives the local weak-gradient identity, and the global \\(L^2\\) bounds give global \\(H^1\\) membership.</p><p>Testing the eigenfunction equation by \\(v_U\\) shows\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega|\\nabla v_U|^2\n=\n\\mu\\int_\\Omega|v_U|^2.\n\\]</p><p>If at least three sign components existed, a nonzero linear combination of two restrictions could be chosen mean zero. Disjoint supports show that it attains the quotient \\(\\mu\\). It is therefore an eigenfunction, but vanishes on the omitted open component, contradicting interior analyticity. Both sign sets are nonempty, so each is connected.</p><p>Four alternating strict signs on the boundary would give a simple arc joining the two positive points, otherwise in the positive interior set, and another joining the negative points in the negative interior set. Such arcs exist by connectedness of open planar sign sets, using short interior end segments and then polygonal paths with loops removed. After applying \\(\\Phi^{-1}\\), their endpoints alternate on a circle. Planar separation forces the two arcs to intersect, contrary to their signs.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 3.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Omega,\\mu,V,\\lambda_D\\) be as in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">For a real vector field \\(X\\in H^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^2)\\) with tangent trace,\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{tr}X\\cdot\\nu=0,\n  \\]\n  define\n  \\[\n  Q(X)\n  =\n  \\int_\\Omega\n  \\bigl(|\\operatorname{div}X|^2+|\\operatorname{curl}X|^2\\bigr),\n  \\qquad\n  \\operatorname{curl}X=\\partial_1X_2-\\partial_2X_1.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every such field satisfies\n\\[\nQ(X)\\ge\\mu\\int_\\Omega|X|^2.\n\\]\nEquality holds exactly when\n\\[\nX=\\nabla v\n\\]\nfor some \\(v\\in V\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Solve\n\\[\n\\Delta v=\\operatorname{div}X,\n\\qquad\n\\partial_\\nu v=0,\n\\qquad\n\\int_\\Omega v=0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\Delta j=\\operatorname{curl}X,\n\\qquad\nj|_{\\partial\\Omega}=0.\n\\]\nThe Neumann compatibility follows from the tangent trace. The solutions belong to \\(H^2(\\Omega)\\) by smooth-boundary regularity. The rotated gradient\n\\[\n\\nabla^\\perp j=(-\\partial_2j,\\partial_1j)\n\\]\nis tangent. Thus\n\\[\nZ=X-\\nabla v-\\nabla^\\perp j\n\\]\nis tangent, curl-free, and divergence-free. Its components are harmonic in the interior, so \\(Z\\) is smooth there. Simple connectivity gives an interior potential \\(r\\) with \\(Z=\\nabla r\\).</p><p>Here \\(r\\in H^1(\\Omega)\\), as can be seen without assuming its global integrability in advance. For \\(M&gt;0\\), its bounded truncation\n\\[\nr_M=\\max(-M,\\min(r,M))\n\\]\nhas local weak gradient of norm at most \\(|Z|\\), hence belongs globally to \\(H^1(\\Omega)\\). Fix an interior ball \\(B\\). Its averages \\((r_M)_B\\) are bounded by \\(\\sup_B|r|\\). The Poincaré inequality, with this fixed-ball average as anchor, gives\n\\[\n\\|r_M\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}\n\\le\nC\\|\\nabla r_M\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}+C|(r_M)_B|\n\\le\nC\\|Z\\|_2+C\\sup_B|r|.\n\\]\nFatou gives \\(r\\in L^2(\\Omega)\\), and its weak gradient is \\(Z\\). Testing \\(\\operatorname{div}Z=0\\) against \\(r\\), with its zero normal trace, yields\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega|\\nabla r|^2=0.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nX=\\nabla v+\\nabla^\\perp j.\n\\]</p><p>The summands are orthogonal in \\(L^2\\), since the second is divergence-free and tangent. Moreover,\n\\[\nQ(X)=\\|\\Delta v\\|_2^2+\\|\\Delta j\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nIntegration by parts and the two Poincaré inequalities show\n\\[\n\\|\\nabla v\\|_2^2\n\\le\n\\|v\\|_2\\|\\Delta v\\|_2\n\\le\n\\mu^{-1/2}\\|\\nabla v\\|_2\\|\\Delta v\\|_2,\n\\]\nand likewise\n\\[\n\\|\\Delta j\\|_2^2\\ge\\lambda_D\\|\\nabla j\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(X)\n&amp;\\ge\n\\mu\\|\\nabla v\\|_2^2+\\lambda_D\\|\\nabla j\\|_2^2\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\mu\\|X\\|_2^2+(\\lambda_D-\\mu)\\|\\nabla j\\|_2^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nProposition 1 gives \\(\\lambda_D&gt;\\mu\\), proving the inequality.</p><p>Equality forces \\(j=0\\) and equality in the Rayleigh inequality for \\(v\\), so \\(v\\in V\\). Conversely, gradients of functions in \\(V\\) give equality directly.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 4.4. The scalar constructions and boundary formula are original Lemmas 4.1–4.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In the general disk-potential setting, let \\(dq=a\\,dx\\), where \\(a\\in L^\\infty(\\mathbb D)\\) is nonnegative, and suppose\n  \\[\n  \\|\\psi\\|_{L^2(q)}^2\n  \\le\n  d\\int_{\\mathbb D}|\\nabla\\psi|^2\n  \\qquad(\\psi\\in H_0^1(\\mathbb D))\n  \\]\n  for some \\(0&lt;d&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the Green function \\(G\\), Poisson kernel \\(P_s\\), operator \\(T\\), and kernels \\(K,R,N\\) defined in Theorem 2. Write\n  \\[\n  N_0(s,t)=\\frac1{\\pi|s-t|^2}.\n  \\]\n  A subscript \\(q\\) on an inner product or norm denotes \\(L^2(q)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For Lipschitz scalar or vector data \\(h\\) on \\(S^1\\), define the harmonic extension\n  \\[\n  Ah(x)=\\int_{S^1}P_s(x)h(s)\\,ds.\n  \\]\n  Let \\(W_h\\) denote the weak solution with trace \\(h\\) of\n  \\[\n  -\\Delta W_h\\,dx=W_h\\,dq,\n  \\]\n  constructed componentwise for vector data.</p><p class=\"li\">For the vector-field application, specialize to the domain \\(\\Omega\\), eigenvalue \\(\\mu\\), eigenspace \\(V\\), and conformal map \\(\\Phi\\) of Proposition 1, and set\n  \\[\n  dq(x)=\\mu|\\Phi'(x)|^2\\,dx.\n  \\]\n  In this specialization the disk inequality holds with \\(d=\\mu/\\lambda_D&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A vector boundary function \\(h\\) is tangent if \\(h(s)\\) is tangent to \\(\\partial\\Omega\\) at \\(\\Phi(s)\\). For tangent Lipschitz \\(h\\), set\n  \\[\n  X_h=W_h\\circ\\Phi^{-1},\n  \\qquad\n  E(h)=Q(X_h)-\\mu\\|X_h\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}^2.\n  \\]\n  Write \\(E(h,k)\\) for the associated bilinear form.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0\\ne u\\in V\\), set\n  \\[\n  g(s)=(\\nabla u)(\\Phi(s)).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>In the general disk-potential setting:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(T\\) is bounded and self-adjoint on \\(L^2(q)\\), with \\(\\|T\\|\\le d&lt;1\\). The same holds with \\(q\\) replaced by any measure \\(0\\le\\rho\\le q\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The solution is uniquely defined and satisfies\n   \\[\n   W_h=Ah+T(I-T)^{-1}Ah.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. \\(K(p,s)\\) is finite, positive, and bounded as a function of \\(s\\) for each fixed \\(p\\). The kernel \\(R\\) is finite off the diagonal, symmetric, nonnegative, and integrable, with\n   \\[\n   \\iint_{S^1\\times S^1}R(s,t)\\,ds\\,dt\n   \\le\n   \\frac{q(\\mathbb D)}{1-d}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. For Lipschitz scalar or vector data,\n   \\[\n   W_h(p)=\\int_{S^1}K(p,s)h(s)\\,ds,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\langle Ah,(I-T)^{-1}Ak\\rangle_q\n   =\n   \\iint_{S^1\\times S^1}R(s,t)h(s)\\cdot k(t)\\,ds\\,dt.\n   \\]</p><p>In the conformal specialization:</p><p class=\"li num\">5. \\(E\\ge0\\) on tangent Lipschitz data, and\n   \\[\n   E(h)=0\n   \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n   X_h=\\nabla v\n   \\text{ for some }v\\in V.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">6. The boundary gradient \\(g\\) satisfies\n   \\[\n   W_g=(\\nabla u)\\circ\\Phi,\n   \\qquad\n   E(g)=0.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">7. For every real Lipschitz function \\(b\\) on \\(S^1\\),\n   \\[\n   E(bg)\n   =\n   \\frac12\n   \\iint_{S^1\\times S^1}\n   N(s,t)(b(s)-b(t))^2g(s)\\cdot g(t)\\,ds\\,dt.\n   \\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The scalar operator, solution, and kernel assertions follow from Lemmas 3.2 and 3.3. The conformal weighted inequality, nonnegativity, equality space, and boundary formula follow from Lemma 3.4.</p><p>Fix \\(0\\ne u\\in V\\) and let\n\\[\ng(s)=(\\nabla u)(\\Phi(s)).\n\\]\nIt is tangent by the Neumann condition. Differentiation of the Euclidean Helmholtz equation commutes with its Cartesian derivatives, so uniqueness of the scalar boundary problem gives\n\\[\nW_g=(\\nabla u)\\circ\\Phi.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(X_g=\\nabla u\\) and \\(E(g)=0\\).</p><p>Since \\(E\\) is positive semidefinite and \\(E(g)=0\\), its bilinear form annihilates \\(g\\): this follows by considering \\(E(g+th)\\) for real \\(t\\). In particular, for a real Lipschitz function \\(b\\),\n\\[\nE(g,b^2g)=0.\n\\]</p><p>Subtract the boundary formula of Lemma 3.4 for this pair from the formula for \\(E(bg,bg)\\). The curvature terms cancel. The harmonic term gives\n\\[\n\\frac12N_0(s,t)(b(s)-b(t))^2g(s)\\cdot g(t)\n\\]\nin the double integral. Symmetry of \\(R\\) gives the same expression with \\(R\\) in place of \\(N_0\\). Their sum is\n\\[\nE(bg)\n=\n\\frac12\n\\iint_{S^1\\times S^1}\nN(s,t)(b(s)-b(t))^2g(s)\\cdot g(t)\\,ds\\,dt.\n\\]\nAll terms are integrable: the squared Lipschitz difference cancels the singularity of \\(N_0\\), and \\(R\\) is integrable.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Omega\\) be the smooth bounded simply connected planar domain used in the conformal specialization of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X\\in H^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^2)\\) have tangent trace.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\tau\\) be the counterclockwise unit tangent to \\(\\partial\\Omega\\), and put\n  \\[\n  \\kappa=\\det(\\tau,\\partial_\\tau\\tau).\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(\\kappa\\) is positive on a convex circle.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the divergence–curl energy \\(Q\\) of Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nQ(X)\n=\n\\int_\\Omega|\\nabla X|^2\n+\n\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}\\kappa|X|^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For smooth fields, the difference of the bulk integrands is \\(2\\det\\nabla X\\). Its integral is the boundary pairing\n\\[\n\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}\n\\bigl(X_1\\partial_\\tau X_2-X_2\\partial_\\tau X_1\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>This identity extends to \\(H^1\\) fields by smooth approximation, since traces are in \\(H^{1/2}\\) and their tangential derivatives are in \\(H^{-1/2}\\). If the trace is \\(a\\tau\\), the pairing is\n\\[\n\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}\\kappa a^2.\n\\]\nThis follows first for smooth \\(a\\) and then by approximation in \\(H^{1/2}\\). It gives the stated identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the general disk-potential setting of Proposition 3: \\(dq=a\\,dx\\), \\(a\\in L^\\infty(\\mathbb D)\\), \\(a\\ge0\\), and the weighted Dirichlet inequality with \\(0&lt;d&lt;1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The operator\n\\[\nTf(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb D}G(x,y)f(y)\\,dq(y)\n\\]\nis a bounded self-adjoint operator on \\(L^2(q)\\), with\n\\[\n\\|T\\|\\le d&lt;1.\n\\]\nThe same statement holds with \\(q\\) replaced by any measure \\(0\\le\\rho\\le q\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(dq=a\\,dx\\). For \\(f\\in L^2(q)\\), the source \\(af\\) belongs to \\(L^2(dx)\\), since\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb D}|af|^2\\,dx\n\\le\n\\|a\\|_\\infty\\int_{\\mathbb D}|f|^2\\,dq.\n\\]\nIts Dirichlet solution \\(\\psi=Tf\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb D}|\\nabla\\psi|^2=\\langle\\psi,f\\rangle_q.\n\\]\nCombining this with the weighted Dirichlet inequality gives\n\\[\n\\|\\psi\\|_q^2\\le d\\|\\psi\\|_q\\|f\\|_q,\n\\]\nhence the norm bound.</p><p>Symmetry follows from the Green kernel, or by testing the two Poisson equations against each other. The Green formula can first be proved for smooth compactly supported sources and then passed to \\(L^2(dx)\\) by approximation; \\(G(x,\\cdot)\\in L^2(dx)\\) gives pointwise evaluation. These sections vary continuously in \\(L^2\\) on compact interior sets, so this is the continuous interior representative.</p><p>For \\(\\rho\\le q\\), its density remains bounded and its weighted Dirichlet inequality follows from that for \\(q\\), proving the same result.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.2, together with the preceding solution construction (4.5)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the general disk-potential setting and the objects \\(T,A,W_h,K,R,N\\) of Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For Lipschitz scalar or vector boundary data \\(h\\), the unique weak solution is\n\\[\nW_h=Ah+T(I-T)^{-1}Ah.\n\\]\nThe kernels \\(K,R,N\\) are finite at their stated points. For fixed \\(p\\), \\(K(p,\\cdot)\\) is positive and bounded. The kernel \\(R\\) is symmetric, nonnegative, and integrable, with\n\\[\n\\iint_{S^1\\times S^1}R(s,t)\\,ds\\,dt\n\\le\n\\frac{q(\\mathbb D)}{1-d}.\n\\]\nFor Lipschitz data \\(h,k\\),\n\\[\nW_h(p)=\\int_{S^1}K(p,s)h(s)\\,ds,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\langle Ah,(I-T)^{-1}Ak\\rangle_q\n=\n\\iint_{S^1\\times S^1}\nR(s,t)h(s)\\cdot k(t)\\,ds\\,dt.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The harmonic extension \\(Ah\\) belongs to \\(H^1(\\mathbb D)\\). By Lemma 3.2, \\(I-T\\) is invertible on \\(L^2(q)\\). The correction\n\\[\nT(I-T)^{-1}Ah\n\\]\nhas zero trace and solves the required Poisson equation. Thus\n\\[\nW_h=Ah+T(I-T)^{-1}Ah.\n\\]\nUniqueness follows from the weighted Dirichlet inequality. The construction is componentwise for vectors.</p><p>The elementary estimates\n\\[\nP_s(y)\\le\\frac{C}{|y-s|},\n\\qquad\n0\\le G(x,y)\\le C\\log\\frac2{|x-y|}\n\\]\ngive uniform bounds for \\(P_s\\) in \\(L^{3/2}(dx)\\) and for \\(G(x,\\cdot)\\) in \\(L^3(dx)\\). Hölder and the bounded density imply\n\\[\n\\sup_{s\\in S^1}\\|TP_s\\|_{L^\\infty(\\mathbb D)}&lt;\\infty.\n\\]</p><p>The section \\(P_s\\) need not belong to \\(L^2(q)\\), but one Green integration gives\n\\[\nF_s=TP_s\\in L^\\infty(\\mathbb D)\\subset L^2(q).\n\\]\nWe can now apply the \\(L^2(q)\\) contraction estimate to the remaining iterates. For \\(n\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|(T^nP_s)(p)|\n&amp;=\n|\\langle G(p,\\cdot),T^{n-2}F_s\\rangle_q|\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n\\|G(p,\\cdot)\\|_q\\,d^{n-2}\\|F_s\\|_q\n\\le\nCd^{n-2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe constants can be chosen uniformly in \\(p\\) and \\(s\\). This proves the assertion about \\(K\\).</p><p>For distinct \\(s,t\\), the product \\(P_sP_t\\) is integrable: near either boundary pole, the other factor is bounded, and \\(|y-s|^{-1}\\) is locally integrable in two dimensions. The terms with \\(n\\ge1\\) in the definition of \\(R\\) are summable by the first smoothing estimate and the geometric estimate above, since \\(\\int P_s\\,dq\\) is uniformly bounded.</p><p>Reversing the nonnegative chains proves symmetry. Tonelli’s theorem and\n\\[\n\\int_{S^1}P_s(x)\\,ds=1\n\\]\ngive\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\iint_{S^1\\times S^1}R(s,t)\\,ds\\,dt\n&amp;=\n\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\langle1,T^n1\\rangle_q\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n\\sum_{n\\ge0}d^n\\|1\\|_q^2\n=\n\\frac{q(\\mathbb D)}{1-d}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nDiagonal values of \\(R\\) play no role in these boundary integrals.</p><p>Expanding the Neumann series in the solution formula and using the positive kernels gives the two representation identities. The same bounds justify the exchanges for signed data by absolute domination.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "Lemma 4.3, with the nonnegativity and equality-space statement (4.13)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the conformal specialization of Proposition 3:\n  \\[\n  dq=\\mu|\\Phi'|^2\\,dx.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(h,k:S^1\\to\\mathbb R^2\\) be tangent Lipschitz data.</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\kappa\\) from Lemma 3.1 and \\(E\\) from Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The form \\(E\\) is nonnegative on tangent Lipschitz data, and\n\\[\nE(h)=0\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nX_h=\\nabla v\n\\text{ for some }v\\in V.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nE(h,k)\n={}&amp;\n\\frac12\n\\iint_{S^1\\times S^1}\nN_0(s,t)(h(s)-h(t))\\cdot(k(s)-k(t))\\,ds\\,dt\\\\\n&amp;-\n\\iint_{S^1\\times S^1}\nR(s,t)h(s)\\cdot k(t)\\,ds\\,dt\\\\\n&amp;+\n\\int_{S^1}|\\Phi'|(\\kappa\\circ\\Phi)\\,h\\cdot k\\,ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Conformal invariance of the Dirichlet integral and the Dirichlet inequality on \\(\\Omega\\) give\n\\[\n\\|\\psi\\|_{L^2(q)}^2\n\\le\n\\frac{\\mu}{\\lambda_D}\n\\int_{\\mathbb D}|\\nabla\\psi|^2,\n\\qquad\n\\psi\\in H_0^1(\\mathbb D).\n\\]\nProposition 1 gives \\(\\mu/\\lambda_D&lt;1\\), so the preceding scalar constructions apply.</p><p>The field \\(X_h=W_h\\circ\\Phi^{-1}\\) belongs to \\(H^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^2)\\) and has tangent trace. Proposition 2 therefore gives \\(E(h)\\ge0\\), with the stated equality case.</p><p>By Lemma 3.1 and conformal invariance of the scalar Dirichlet integral applied to each Cartesian component, the bulk terms of \\(E(h,k)\\) are\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb D}\\nabla W_h:\\nabla W_k\n-\n\\langle W_h,W_k\\rangle_q.\n\\]\nThe equation for \\(W_k\\) permits replacing \\(W_h\\) here by \\(Ah\\), since their difference has zero trace. Harmonicity of \\(Ah\\) then replaces \\(W_k\\) by \\(Ak\\) in the gradient term. Since\n\\[\nW_k=(I-T)^{-1}Ak\n\\quad\\text{in }L^2(q),\n\\]\nthis gives\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb D}\\nabla Ah:\\nabla Ak\n-\n\\langle Ah,(I-T)^{-1}Ak\\rangle_q.\n\\]\nThe boundary curvature term changes by the factor \\(|\\Phi'|\\), and Lemma 3.3 identifies the second term.</p><p>For the harmonic energy,\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb D}\\nabla Ah:\\nabla Ak\n=\n\\frac12\n\\iint_{S^1\\times S^1}\nN_0(s,t)(h(s)-h(t))\\cdot(k(s)-k(t))\\,ds\\,dt.\n\\]\nIndeed both sides diagonalize in Fourier modes. The Hermitian energy of \\(s^m\\) is \\(2\\pi|m|\\), while\n\\[\n\\iint_{S^1\\times S^1}\n\\frac{|s^m-t^m|^2}{|s-t|^2}\\,ds\\,dt\n=\n(2\\pi)^2|m|\n\\]\nby a geometric sum, verifying the normalization. Smooth convolution approximation, with uniformly bounded Lipschitz constants and convergence in \\(H^{1/2}\\), extends the identity to the stated data. This proves the boundary formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 5.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be the Dirichlet Green function of \\(\\mathbb D\\) defined in Theorem 2.</p><p class=\"li\">A real symmetric matrix has property \\((P)\\) if it has at most one positive eigenvalue, counted with multiplicity.</p><p class=\"li\">Reciprocals of kernels and matrices below are entrywise, never matrix inverses.</p><p class=\"li\">Property \\((P)\\) is preserved by positive diagonal congruence, subtraction of a positive semidefinite matrix, passage to principal submatrices, and limits of matrices of fixed size.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are bounded continuous strictly positive kernels\n\\[\nC_\\epsilon:\\mathbb D\\times\\mathbb D\\longrightarrow(0,\\infty),\n\\qquad 0&lt;\\epsilon\\le1,\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\nC_\\epsilon\\uparrow G\n\\qquad\\text{as }\\epsilon\\downarrow0,\n\\]\nincluding on the diagonal, and every finite matrix\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac1{C_\\epsilon(x_i,x_j)}\\right)_{i,j}\n\\]\nhas property \\((P)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nh(x,y)=\\frac{|x-y|^2}{(1-|x|^2)(1-|y|^2)},\n\\]\n\\[\nL(h)=\\frac1{\\log(1+1/h)}\n\\quad(h&gt;0),\n\\qquad\nL(0)=0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nf(h)=h+\\frac12-L(h).\n\\]</p><p>After multiplying the \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-entries by \\(1-|x|^2\\) and \\(1-|y|^2\\), respectively, the kernel \\(h+\\frac12\\) becomes\n\\[\n\\frac12(1+|x|^2)(1+|y|^2)-2x\\cdot y.\n\\]\nThis is a positive rank-one kernel minus a positive semidefinite kernel, so \\(h+\\frac12\\) has \\((P)\\) on finite sets.</p><p>Since\n\\[\n\\frac1G=4\\pi L(h),\n\\]\nthe identity \\(L=(h+\\frac12)-f\\) will be useful once \\(f(h(x,y))\\) is shown to be positive semidefinite.</p><p>For \\(h&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nL(h)=\\int_0^1h^{1-\\alpha}(h+1)^\\alpha\\,d\\alpha.\n\\]\nFor \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1\\), let \\(c_\\alpha\\) normalize\n\\[\nv^{\\alpha-1}(1+v)^{-1}\\,dv\n\\]\nto a probability measure on \\((0,\\infty)\\), and set \\(r=(1+v)^{-1}\\). Denote expectation with respect to this measure by \\(\\mathbb E_\\alpha\\). Integration by parts gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\alpha(1-r)\n=\nc_\\alpha\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{v^\\alpha}{(1+v)^2}\\,dv\n=\n\\alpha.\n\\]\nFor \\(h&gt;0\\), scaling \\(v\\) in the normalized integral yields\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nh^{1-\\alpha}(h+1)^\\alpha\n&amp;=\nc_\\alpha\n\\int_0^\\infty\n\\frac{h(h+1)}{(h+1)+vh}\\,v^{\\alpha-1}\\,dv\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\mathbb E_\\alpha\\frac{h(h+1)}{h+r}\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\mathbb E_\\alpha\n\\left[\nh+(1-r)-\\frac{r(1-r)}{h+r}\n\\right].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last equality remains true at \\(h=0\\). Integrating in \\(\\alpha\\) therefore gives\n\\[\nf(h)\n=\n\\int_0^1\n\\mathbb E_\\alpha\\frac{r(1-r)}{h+r}\\,d\\alpha,\n\\qquad\n0\\le f(h)\\le f(0)=\\frac12.\n\\]</p><p>Now\n\\[\nk(x,y)=\\frac1{1+h(x,y)}\n=\n\\frac{(1-|x|^2)(1-|y|^2)}{|1-x\\overline y|^2}\n\\]\nis positive semidefinite. Indeed \\((1-x\\overline y)^{-1}\\) is a Gram kernel by its power series; multiplying it by its complex conjugate and the positive diagonal factors preserves positive semidefiniteness.</p><p>For fixed \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\frac1{h+r}\n=\n\\sum_{n\\ge0}(1-r)^n k^{n+1},\n\\]\nwhere powers are entrywise. This series converges even when \\(h=0\\). Each power is positive semidefinite, as follows by taking tensor products of Gram vectors. Thus \\(1/(h+r)\\) is positive semidefinite, and the positive mixture above proves that \\(f(h(x,y))\\) is positive semidefinite. The mixture has finite entries by the displayed bound, including on the diagonal.</p><p>Define\n\\[\n\\frac1{C_\\epsilon(x,y)}\n=\n4\\pi\\left[h+\\frac12-(1-\\epsilon)f(h)\\right]\n=\n4\\pi[L(h)+\\epsilon f(h)].\n\\]\nIts reciprocal matrix has \\((P)\\) by the rank-one-minus-positive-semidefinite representation of \\(h+\\frac12\\) and the positive semidefiniteness of \\(f\\).</p><p>Its denominator is at least \\(2\\pi\\epsilon\\), because\n\\[\nL+\\epsilon f\n=\n\\epsilon\\left(h+\\frac12\\right)+(1-\\epsilon)L\n\\ge\\frac\\epsilon2.\n\\]\nIt is positive and continuous, so \\(C_\\epsilon\\) is bounded, continuous, and strictly positive. Since \\(f\\ge0\\), these kernels increase to \\(G\\), and \\(0&lt;C_\\epsilon\\le G\\). On the diagonal,\n\\[\nC_\\epsilon(x,x)=\\frac1{2\\pi\\epsilon},\n\\]\nas required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma 5.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M\\) be a real symmetric finite matrix with strictly positive entries.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose its entrywise reciprocal has property \\((P)\\), meaning at most one positive eigenvalue.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an index \\(z\\) and a real number \\(c\\ge0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The matrix\n\\[\n\\widetilde M_{ij}=M_{ij}+cM_{iz}M_{zj}\n\\]\nalso has an entrywise reciprocal with property \\((P)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Positive diagonal congruence transforms the reciprocal of \\(M\\) into\n\\[\nH_{ij}=\\frac{M_{iz}M_{zj}}{M_{ij}}.\n\\]\nWriting \\(a=M_{zz}&gt;0\\), its \\(z\\)-row and column are identically \\(a\\). Put \\(z\\) last, and let \\(H_0\\) denote the remaining principal block. Subtracting the last row from every other row, and doing the same to the columns, gives the congruence\n\\[\nH\\sim\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nH_0-a11^T&amp;0\\\\\n0&amp;a\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(a&gt;0\\) and \\(H\\) has \\((P)\\), the first block is nonpositive. Hence\n\\[\nU=a11^T-H\\succeq0.\n\\]\nSince \\(H_{ii}&gt;0\\), one has \\(0\\le U_{ii}&lt;a\\), and positive-semidefinite Cauchy–Schwarz gives \\(|U_{ij}|&lt;a\\).</p><p>Using the same diagonal normalization, the new reciprocal has entries\n\\[\n\\frac{H_{ij}}{1+cH_{ij}}.\n\\]\nThe difference from the constant rank-one kernel is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a}{1+ca}\n-\n\\frac{H_{ij}}{1+cH_{ij}}\n&amp;=\n\\frac{U_{ij}}{(1+ca)(1+ca-cU_{ij})}\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\sum_{n\\ge0}\n\\frac{c^nU_{ij}^{\\,n+1}}{(1+ca)^{n+2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe series converges absolutely because \\(|cU_{ij}|&lt;1+ca\\). Its coefficients are nonnegative and every entrywise power of \\(U\\) is positive semidefinite, even if \\(U\\) has negative entries.</p><p>Thus the normalized reciprocal of \\(\\widetilde M\\) is a positive rank-one kernel minus a positive semidefinite matrix, proving \\((P)\\). For \\(c=0\\), the identity reduces directly to \\(U\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 5.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(dq=a\\,dx\\) on \\(\\mathbb D\\), where \\(a\\in L^\\infty(\\mathbb D)\\) is nonnegative and\n  \\[\n  \\int_{\\mathbb D}|\\psi|^2\\,dq\n  \\le\n  d\\int_{\\mathbb D}|\\nabla\\psi|^2\n  \\qquad(\\psi\\in H_0^1(\\mathbb D))\n  \\]\n  for some \\(0&lt;d&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0\\le\\rho\\le q\\) be supported on a compact subset of \\(\\mathbb D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T_\\rho\\) be the Green operator on \\(L^2(\\rho)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  M^\\rho(x,y)\n  =\n  G(x,y)\n  +\n  \\left\\langle\n  G(x,\\cdot),(I-T_\\rho)^{-1}G(y,\\cdot)\n  \\right\\rangle_\\rho.\n  \\]\n  It is finite for distinct interior points and has infinite diagonal.</p><p class=\"li\">Property \\((P)\\) means that a real symmetric matrix has at most one positive eigenvalue.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every finite set of interior points, the entrywise reciprocal matrix of \\(M^\\rho\\), with diagonal zero, has property \\((P)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First fix \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\) and replace \\(G\\) throughout the definition of \\(M^\\rho\\) by \\(C=C_\\epsilon\\) from Proposition 4. Its integral operator \\(T_C\\) on \\(L^2(\\rho)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|T_Cf|\\le T_\\rho|f|,\n\\qquad\n\\|T_C\\|\\le d&lt;1,\n\\]\nusing Proposition 3. The absolute pointwise domination proves the norm bound without requiring a separate positivity assertion about its quadratic form.</p><p>Partition a compact integration set into finitely many measurable cells with uniformly vanishing diameters. For each cell of positive \\(\\rho\\)-mass, choose a representative, and let \\(\\pi\\) send that cell to the representative. Use its mass as the quadrature weight. Uniform continuity gives uniform convergence of\n\\[\nC(\\pi(\\cdot),\\pi(\\cdot))\\longrightarrow C\n\\]\non the integration set up to null sets.</p><p>The associated operators \\(T_\\pi\\) on \\(L^2(\\rho)\\) converge to \\(T_C\\) in operator norm; for example, their norm difference is at most \\(\\rho(\\mathbb D)\\) times the uniform kernel error. Likewise,\n\\[\nC(x,\\pi(\\cdot))\\longrightarrow C(x,\\cdot)\n\\]\nin \\(L^2(\\rho)\\) for each fixed evaluation point \\(x\\). In particular, sufficiently fine quadratures have \\(\\|T_\\pi\\|&lt;1\\), and\n\\[\nC(x,y)\n+\n\\left\\langle\nC(x,\\pi(\\cdot)),\n(I-T_\\pi)^{-1}C(y,\\pi(\\cdot))\n\\right\\rangle_\\rho\n\\]\nconverges to the regularized continuum resolvent evaluation. This follows from convergence of the source vectors and of the inverses in operator norm.</p><p>For clarity, if the nodes and weights are \\(z_\\alpha,w_\\alpha\\), set\n\\[\n\\mathbf T_{\\alpha\\beta}\n=\n\\sqrt{w_\\alpha}\\,C(z_\\alpha,z_\\beta)\\sqrt{w_\\beta},\n\\qquad\n(\\xi_x)_\\alpha=\\sqrt{w_\\alpha}\\,C(x,z_\\alpha).\n\\]\nThe normalized cell indicators form an orthonormal basis for the step functions. On this subspace \\(T_\\pi\\) is represented by \\(\\mathbf T\\), and it vanishes on the orthogonal complement. Thus \\(\\|\\mathbf T\\|&lt;1\\), and the correction in the discrete expression is\n\\[\n\\xi_x^T(I-\\mathbf T)^{-1}\\xi_y.\n\\]\nEvaluation points enter only these finite vectors, including when they coincide with nodes.</p><p>We next prove \\((P)\\) for each sufficiently fine quadrature. On the union of the evaluation points and quadrature nodes, start with\n\\[\nM_{ij}=C(x_i,x_j).\n\\]\nBy Proposition 4, its reciprocal has \\((P)\\).</p><p>Expanding the finite resolvent sums all chains, multiplying kernel entries along each chain and the quadrature weight at every intermediate occurrence. These nonnegative sums are finite by the preceding matrix formula. Restricting the allowed intermediate nodes gives a subseries and hence remains finite.</p><p>Suppose some nodes have been allowed and their chain sum is \\(M\\). Admit one more node \\(z\\) of weight \\(w&gt;0\\). Chains with exactly \\(k\\ge1\\) internal occurrences of \\(z\\) have total weight\n\\[\nw^kM_{iz}M_{zz}^{k-1}M_{zj}.\n\\]\nAll factors are positive. Finiteness of the full series forces \\(wM_{zz}&lt;1\\), and summing over \\(k\\) updates the matrix to\n\\[\nM_{ij}\n+\n\\frac{w}{1-wM_{zz}}M_{iz}M_{zj}.\n\\]\nProposition 5 therefore propagates \\((P)\\) one node at a time. This counting remains valid when an endpoint is a quadrature node, since only internal occurrences receive weights. Coincident node locations may be merged by adding their weights.</p><p>Thus the discrete kernel has the required reciprocal property. The quadrature limit gives it for the regularized continuum kernel. If \\(\\rho=0\\), this conclusion follows directly from Proposition 4.</p><p>Finally let \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\). Every chain integral increases to its counterpart with \\(G\\) by monotone convergence, as does the sum of these nonnegative integrals. Off the diagonal, its limit is \\(M^\\rho\\), and the correction is finite because\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\left\\langle\nG(x,\\cdot),(I-T_\\rho)^{-1}G(y,\\cdot)\n\\right\\rangle_\\rho\n\\right|\n\\le\n\\frac{\\|G(x,\\cdot)\\|_\\rho\\|G(y,\\cdot)\\|_\\rho}{1-d}.\n\\]\nThe direct diagonal term tends to infinity, so its reciprocal tends to zero. Finite entrywise limits preserve \\((P)\\), completing the proof.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Lemma 5.6. The restriction to a codimension-one subspace is the algebraic step in the proof of original Theorem 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(dq=a\\,dx\\), where \\(a\\in L^\\infty(\\mathbb D)\\) is nonnegative and satisfies the weighted Dirichlet inequality with a constant \\(0&lt;d&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K,N\\) be the kernels of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(p\\in\\mathbb D\\) and distinct points \\(s_1,\\ldots,s_m\\in S^1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define the real symmetric matrix \\(B\\) and vector \\(\\ell\\) by\n  \\[\n  B_{ij}\n  =\n  \\begin{cases}\n  1/N(s_i,s_j),&amp;i\\ne j,\\\\\n  0,&amp;i=j,\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\qquad\n  \\ell_i=\\frac1{K(p,s_i)}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Property \\((P)\\) means at most one positive eigenvalue.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The bordered matrix\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nB&amp;\\ell\\\\\n\\ell^T&amp;0\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nhas property \\((P)\\). Consequently,\n\\[\nx^TBx\\le0\n\\qquad\\text{whenever }\\ell^Tx=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix compactly supported \\(0\\le\\rho\\le q\\), and write \\(K^\\rho,R^\\rho,N^\\rho\\) for the kernels constructed with \\(\\rho\\) in place of \\(q\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition 6 to\n\\[\nrs_1,\\ldots,rs_m,p,\n\\qquad r\\uparrow1,\\quad r&gt;|p|.\n\\]\nThe explicit Green formula gives\n\\[\n\\frac{G(rs,x)}{1-r}\\longrightarrow P_s(x),\n\\]\nuniformly on compact interior sets, and\n\\[\n\\frac{G(rs,rt)}{(1-r)^2}\\longrightarrow N_0(s,t)\n\\qquad(s\\ne t).\n\\]\nFor the second limit, one may use\n\\[\nG(rs,rt)\n=\n\\frac1{4\\pi}\n\\log\\left(\n1+\\frac{(1-r^2)^2}{r^2|s-t|^2}\n\\right).\n\\]</p><p>The bounded resolvent in the definition of \\(M^\\rho\\) and the uniform convergence on \\(\\operatorname{supp}\\rho\\) therefore imply\n\\[\n\\frac{M^\\rho(rs_i,rs_j)}{(1-r)^2}\n\\longrightarrow N^\\rho(s_i,s_j)\n\\qquad(i\\ne j),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{M^\\rho(p,rs_i)}{1-r}\n\\longrightarrow K^\\rho(p,s_i).\n\\]\nHere the first resolvent correction tends to\n\\[\n\\langle P_{s_i},(I-T_\\rho)^{-1}P_{s_j}\\rangle_\\rho\n=\nR^\\rho(s_i,s_j).\n\\]\nThe second tends to\n\\[\nP_{s_i}(p)\n+\n\\langle G(p,\\cdot),(I-T_\\rho)^{-1}P_{s_i}\\rangle_\\rho\n=\nK^\\rho(p,s_i).\n\\]\nThese pairings are legitimate on the compact support, where the Poisson kernels are bounded.</p><p>Scale the reciprocal matrix by positive diagonal congruence with factors \\(1-r\\) at the moving points and factor \\(1\\) at \\(p\\). Its limit is the bordered matrix in the statement with \\(K^\\rho,N^\\rho\\), so it has \\((P)\\). The zero diagonal stays zero throughout.</p><p>Now choose increasing disk cutoffs\n\\[\nd\\rho_n=1_{\\{|x|\\le r_n\\}}\\,dq,\n\\qquad\nr_n\\uparrow1.\n\\]\nEvery term of \\(K^{\\rho_n}\\) and \\(R^{\\rho_n}\\) is a nonnegative chain integral. Monotone convergence in each finite product integral and then in the series gives\n\\[\nK^{\\rho_n}(p,s)\\uparrow K(p,s),\n\\qquad\nN^{\\rho_n}(s,t)\\uparrow N(s,t)\n\\quad(s\\ne t).\n\\]\nThese limits are finite by Proposition 3. Taking the reciprocals and the finite matrix limit proves the bordered inertia assertion. No \\(L^2(q)\\) boundary limit of an individual Poisson kernel has been used.</p><p>Since each \\(\\ell_i&gt;0\\), Lemma 7.1 applies and proves the asserted inequality on \\(\\ell^\\perp\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "the final algebraic step in the proof of Theorem 5.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B\\) be a real symmetric \\(m\\times m\\) matrix.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0\\ne\\ell\\in\\mathbb R^m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  A=\n  \\begin{pmatrix}\n  B&amp;\\ell\\\\\n  \\ell^T&amp;0\n  \\end{pmatrix}\n  \\]\n  has at most one positive eigenvalue.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nx^TBx\\le0\n\\qquad\\text{for every }x\\in\\mathbb R^m\n\\text{ with }\\ell^Tx=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>If instead \\(x^TBx&gt;0\\), choose \\(y\\) with \\(\\ell^Ty\\ne0\\) and replace it by\n\\[\ny-\\frac{x^TBy}{x^TBx}x.\n\\]\nThen \\(x^TBy=0\\) and still \\(\\ell^Ty\\ne0\\).</p><p>In the bordered form, \\((x,0)\\) and \\((y,c)\\) are orthogonal, and \\(c\\) can be chosen so that\n\\[\n(y,c)^TA(y,c)\n=\ny^TBy+2c\\ell^Ty&gt;0.\n\\]\nThey would span a two-dimensional positive subspace, contradicting the assumed inertia condition.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemma 6.1 and the Hilbert-multiplier argument leading to equation (6.2)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Omega,\\mu,V,\\Phi\\) be as in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  dq=\\mu|\\Phi'|^2\\,dx,\n  \\]\n  and use the kernels \\(K,N\\), solution operator \\(W\\), and boundary form \\(E\\) of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0\\ne u\\in V\\), and set\n  \\[\n  g(s)=(\\nabla u)(\\Phi(s)).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(p\\in\\mathbb D\\), and define\n  \\[\n  D_p(s,t)=\\frac{K(p,s)K(p,t)}{N(s,t)}\n  \\quad(s\\ne t),\n  \\qquad D_p(s,s)=0.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an injective Lipschitz map\n\\[\nb:S^1\\longrightarrow H\n\\]\ninto a separable real Hilbert space such that\n\\[\n\\|b(s)-b(t)\\|_H^2=D_p(s,t).\n\\]\nIf \\(b_j\\) are its coordinates in a finite or countable orthonormal basis of \\(H\\), then each \\(b_j\\) is real Lipschitz and\n\\[\n\\sum_jE(b_jg)\n=\n\\frac12|W_g(p)|^2\n=\n\\frac12|\\nabla u(\\Phi(p))|^2.\n\\]\nIn particular, if \\(\\Phi(p)\\) is a critical point of \\(u\\), then for every \\(j\\) there is \\(v_j\\in V\\) such that\n\\[\n(\\nabla v_j)(\\Phi(s))=b_j(s)g(s)\n\\qquad(s\\in S^1).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(b\\) from Lemma 8.1 and let \\(b_j\\) be its coordinates in a finite or countable orthonormal basis. Each \\(b_j\\) is real Lipschitz, so the multiplier identity of Proposition 3 applies.</p><p>For partial sums, the absolute integrand is bounded by\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(s,t)\\sum_{j\\le m}|b_j(s)-b_j(t)|^2|g(s)||g(t)|\n&amp;\\le\nN(s,t)D_p(s,t)|g(s)||g(t)|\\\\\n&amp;=\nK(p,s)K(p,t)|g(s)||g(t)|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe right-hand side is bounded and integrable. Dominated convergence therefore gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_jE(b_jg)\n&amp;=\n\\frac12\n\\iint_{S^1\\times S^1}\nK(p,s)K(p,t)g(s)\\cdot g(t)\\,ds\\,dt\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\frac12\n\\left|\n\\int_{S^1}K(p,s)g(s)\\,ds\n\\right|^2\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\frac12|W_g(p)|^2\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\frac12|\\nabla u(\\Phi(p))|^2,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhere the last two equalities use Proposition 3.</p><p>If \\(\\Phi(p)\\) is a critical point, the sum is zero. Every summand is nonnegative. By the equality-space assertion of Proposition 3, for every \\(j\\) there is \\(v_j\\in V\\) with\n\\[\nX_{b_jg}=\\nabla v_j.\n\\]\nIts boundary gradient, pulled back by \\(\\Phi\\), is \\(b_jg\\). The trace equality holds pointwise because both sides are continuous.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the conformal disk potential, kernels \\(K,N\\), point \\(p\\), and kernel \\(D_p\\) of Proposition 8.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an injective Lipschitz map\n\\[\nb:S^1\\longrightarrow H\n\\]\ninto a separable real Hilbert space satisfying\n\\[\n\\|b(s)-b(t)\\|_H^2=D_p(s,t).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First obtain conditional negativity directly from Proposition 7. For distinct points \\(s_i\\) and real coefficients \\(a_i\\) with \\(\\sum_i a_i=0\\), put\n\\[\nx_i=K(p,s_i)a_i.\n\\]\nFor the matrix and vector in Proposition 7,\n\\[\n\\ell^Tx=\\sum_i a_i=0,\n\\]\nand therefore\n\\[\n\\sum_{i,j}a_i a_jD_p(s_i,s_j)=x^TBx\\le0.\n\\]\nRepeated points are handled by combining their coefficients.</p><p>Fix \\(s_0\\in S^1\\). Conditional negativity makes the anchored kernel\n\\[\n\\Gamma(s,t)\n=\n\\frac12\\bigl(D_p(s,s_0)+D_p(t,s_0)-D_p(s,t)\\bigr)\n\\]\npositive semidefinite: append to any finite family of coefficients the coefficient at \\(s_0\\) that makes their sum zero.</p><p>Give formal finite linear combinations of symbols \\(b(s)\\) the bilinear form with Gram kernel \\(\\Gamma\\), quotient by its nullspace, and complete. Since \\(D_p(s,s)=0\\), this realizes\n\\[\n\\|b(s)-b(t)\\|_H^2=D_p(s,t)\n\\]\nand \\(b(s_0)=0\\).</p><p>Positivity of \\(K\\) and finiteness and positivity of \\(N\\) off the diagonal give \\(D_p(s,t)&gt;0\\) for \\(s\\ne t\\), so \\(b\\) is injective. Also \\(N\\ge N_0\\) gives\n\\[\n\\|b(s)-b(t)\\|_H^2\n\\le\n\\pi\\|K(p,\\cdot)\\|_\\infty^2|s-t|^2.\n\\]\nThus \\(b\\) is Lipschitz.</p><p>Replace \\(H\\) by the closed span of \\(b(S^1)\\); it is separable by continuity and separability of the circle.</p>"}], "edges": [[2, 0, "cite"], [2, 7, "cite"], [2, 8, "cite"], [2, 12, "cite"], [2, 18, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [3, 5, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 2, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 2, "cite"], [7, 9, "cite"], [7, 12, "cite"], [8, 1, "cite"], [8, 15, "cite"], [8, 16, "cite"], [8, 18, "cite"], [9, 12, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [11, 8, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 8, "cite"], [13, 15, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 1, "cite"], [16, 19, "cite"], [17, 16, "cite"], [18, 0, "cite"], [19, 18, "cite"]]});
